## 1. Open Investigation: `enrich-safety-investigation` — Schema Drift in a Shared Lookup Source

This worker (Acme Oy, orders trigger) has had an **open investigation since 2026-01-26** — over two months. Its last run failed with `field_not_in_source: price_list.Article`. The `price_list` source no longer has an `Article` column; it now contains `Item code` and `VAT rate`. The worker has not run since.

**This is a blast-radius warning, not just a single-worker issue.** All 18 enrichment workers share the identical model definition (same digest) and all depend on a `price_list` lookup keyed on `Article`. The other 17 enrichment workers are still running successfully (last runs through mid-February), which means the schema change has so far only hit the Acme Oy orders data path. But if this `price_list` schema change propagates to other customers or triggers, up to 18 workers could fail simultaneously with the same error. The investigation should determine whether this is an isolated Acme Oy data issue or a structural change to `price_list` that will cascade — and it has been open long enough that it warrants prompt resolution.

## 2. Severe Shared-Dependency Concentration (Structural Risk — Nothing Currently Failing Beyond the One Investigation)

This 70-worker fleet is highly concentrated on multiple shared dependencies. Per your preferences, I am flagging this as structural risk even though the fleet is mostly healthy:

- **Engine (execution harness):** 4 harnesses, each serving 17–18 workers (24–26%). A bug or breaking change in any one harness takes down all workers on it.
- **Trigger (input source):** 6 trigger paths, each shared by 11–12 workers (16–17%). A malformed file or inbox issue in any path affects all workers reading from it.
- **Model definition (digest):** Only **2 distinct digests** across the entire fleet — one shared by **53 workers (76%)**, one by 17 (24%). The 53-worker digest covers all aggregation, enrichment, and reconciliation workers. A single model change could affect the majority of the fleet at once.
- **Effect target:** All 17 reservation workers write to `append_to_reservations` — a single shared state target. A corruption or locking issue there blocks all 17 committing workers.

The fleet is one change away from a fleet-wide problem on every one of these axes.

## 3. All 17 Reservation Workers Are Functionally Inert — 100% Refusal, Zero Effects Ever Applied

Every reservation worker (rese-sf-53 through rese-sf-69) has refused 100% of requests across all 6 runs. They all request the same date (`2026-05-05`) and all receive `ALREADY_RESERVED`. The reservation state is stuck at 4 and never changes. No reservation has ever been successfully booked by any worker in this cohort.

These workers report status "ok" (no exceptions), so this pattern is invisible if you only look at exception counts or per-worker health. This looks like either stale/repeated input data (same date every run) or a permanently full shared reservation state. Worth investigating whether the input is stuck or the reservation target is misconfigured.

## 4. Suggested System Improvements

- **Proactive schema-drift detection for shared lookup sources.** The `price_list` schema change was caught by one worker, but 18 workers depend on that source. The system should warn when a shared dependency changes schema, before it cascades.
- **Alert on perpetual refusal patterns.** 17 workers refusing 100% of the time for their entire lifetime should trigger a systemic alert, not show as "ok." The current health model treats "refused but no exception" as healthy, which hides functionally dead workers.
- **Consider dependency diversification.** Workers serving different customers could benefit from isolated input paths or independently versioned harnesses, to reduce the blast radius of any single change.
